how does Junoon see itself as drawing from

00:09:22.050 --> 00:09:23.730
that huge spectrum of food?

00:09:23.730 --> 00:09:25.225
- [Akshay Bhardwaj] What
we try to definitely do

00:09:25.225 --> 00:09:28.697
with the menu is utilize
different regions of India.

00:09:28.697 --> 00:09:30.993
We have coastal dishes which
have a lot of seafood in it.

00:09:30.993 --> 00:09:32.964
You have northern dishes,
you have southern dishes.

00:09:32.964 --> 00:09:35.076
The Butter Pepper Shrimp
is more of a coastal dish.

00:09:35.076 --> 00:09:37.854
It's a take on a very traditional
dish in Mumbai, actually.

00:09:37.854 --> 00:09:40.221
- And is the shank inspired
by a particular region?

00:09:40.221 --> 00:09:41.054
- [Akshay Bhardwaj] Yeah.

00:09:41.054 --> 00:09:42.639
You have cumin power, coriander, masala,

00:09:42.639 --> 00:09:46.130
that's definitely more of a
north Indian style of cooking.

00:09:46.130 --> 00:09:47.940
So with the butter pepper garlic shrimp,

00:09:47.940 --> 00:09:49.603
whenever a guest comes and asks me,

00:09:49.603 --> 00:09:50.589
well, what goes into it?

00:09:50.589 --> 00:09:52.641
I say, well, you actually
read the name of the dish,

00:09:52.641 --> 00:09:54.673
it's literally those things
that go into the dish.

00:09:54.673 --> 00:09:56.932
We'll sear the shrimps, we'll saute it.

00:09:56.932 --> 00:09:57.833
We'll add the sauce to it.

00:09:57.833 --> 00:09:59.535
And then we'll pair that with

00:09:59.535 --> 00:10:01.054
a traditional Indian bread called Pao.

00:10:01.054 --> 00:10:02.057
Guests were actually complaining

00:10:02.057 --> 00:10:04.095
the sauce was so good and so addicting

00:10:04.095 --> 00:10:06.105
that they wanted to eat it with something.

00:10:06.105 --> 00:10:07.822
So that's when we decided
we'll pair this bread.

00:10:07.822 --> 00:10:09.925
- [Andrew] Let's try a little
bit of the sauce first,

00:10:09.925 --> 00:10:11.535
and then we'll take a shrimp each.

00:10:11.535 --> 00:10:12.620
- Always with the strategy.

00:10:12.620 --> 00:10:14.885
- I'm a very strategic eater.

00:10:14.885 --> 00:10:16.477
- All I think about when I have food

00:10:16.477 --> 00:10:19.847
is how I get it from the
plate to my mouth the fastest.

00:10:19.847 --> 00:10:20.680
- Okay.

00:10:20.680 --> 00:10:21.513
- Alright.

00:10:23.888 --> 00:10:26.392
I understand why this bread is here.

00:10:26.392 --> 00:10:27.225
- [Andrew] Wanna try a shrimp?

00:10:27.225 --> 00:10:28.725
- [Steven] Shrimp!

00:10:29.850 --> 00:10:32.084
There is this flavor
that is still lingering

00:10:32.084 --> 00:10:33.016
in the back of my mouth.

00:10:33.016 --> 00:10:35.426
It's like a really peppery fiery taste,

00:10:35.426 --> 00:10:36.886
just living back here.

00:10:36.886 --> 00:10:38.229
- [Andrew] Then you take
a sip of the cocktail,

00:10:38.229 --> 00:10:39.867
and it washes it all away.

00:10:39.867 --> 00:10:41.455
- [Steven] Oh I likey like that.

00:10:41.455 --> 00:10:42.531
- (laughs) Yeah.

00:10:42.531 --> 00:10:43.698
- Pao, oh man!

00:10:44.398 --> 00:10:45.981
That looks so good.

00:10:47.669 --> 00:10:48.996
- You know that makes me say?

00:10:48.996 --> 00:10:49.829
Pao!

00:10:49.891 --> 00:10:52.531
- So with the shank, we
first braise the lamb shank.

00:10:52.531 --> 00:10:54.988
And we actually use that as a cooking oil.

00:10:54.988 --> 00:10:56.680
Then we'll add our onions to it.

00:10:56.680 --> 00:10:58.573
And then we make something
called the Putli,

00:10:58.573 --> 00:11:01.229
which is we take a pull of whole spices

00:11:01.229 --> 00:11:03.394
from the spice room and we
wrap it in cheese cloth.

00:11:03.394 --> 00:11:04.689
We throw it into the pot,

00:11:04.689 --> 00:11:05.928
add some ginger and garlic.

00:11:05.928 --> 00:11:08.117
We'll add some cumin
powder, coriander powder,

00:11:08.117 --> 00:11:09.454
red chili powder, turmeric,

00:11:09.454 --> 00:11:11.420
as well as our Garam Masala spice blend.

00:11:11.420 --> 00:11:13.391
And then we'll add the
shanks and lotus pods.

00:11:13.391 --> 00:11:16.624
Definitely adds a little creamy
texture to the dish overall.

00:11:16.624 --> 00:11:19.103
My favorite bread to eat is
definitely the Laccha Paratha,

00:11:19.103 --> 00:11:20.793
which is basically just whole wheat flour,

00:11:20.793 --> 00:11:23.437
we'll baste it with a little
bit of butter and oil,

00:11:23.437 --> 00:11:26.076
stretch it out, so make it
about seven to ten folds.

00:11:26.076 --> 00:11:28.307
And each layer, the top
layer will be the crunchiest,

00:11:28.307 --> 00:11:31.110
the bottom later will be less
and less crunchy as it goes.

00:11:31.110 --> 00:11:32.736
And then we'll put it in the tandoori.

00:11:32.736 --> 00:11:35.186
It's so enjoyable to eat
with any curry, with any,

00:11:35.186 --> 00:11:36.769
eat on it own also.

00:11:37.765 --> 00:11:39.675
- [Andrew] Here we are
Steven, to the main event.

00:11:39.675 --> 00:11:40.925
The Lamb Shank.

00:11:41.016 --> 00:11:41.986
- What's your strategy here?

00:11:41.986 --> 00:11:43.647
Is it potato first, is it the bone?

00:11:43.647 --> 00:11:44.874
- It's sauce first.

00:11:44.874 --> 00:11:47.138
I definitely wouldn't recommend
starting with the bone.

00:11:47.138 --> 00:11:48.388
- [Steven] Woo!

00:11:48.467 --> 00:11:49.300
- [Andrew] Look at that!

00:11:49.300 --> 00:11:50.357
- You almost think for a second

00:11:50.357 --> 00:11:51.779
that you could walk on water

00:11:51.779 --> 00:11:53.546
when you put your knife on there.

00:11:53.546 --> 00:11:54.379
It still holds the tension.

00:11:54.379 --> 00:11:55.712
- It's so thick.

00:11:57.585 --> 00:11:58.717
- That is so good.

00:11:58.717 --> 00:12:01.717
- The spices are so vibrant in this.

00:12:01.746 --> 00:12:02.897
- You're gonna to the
Laccha Paratha first?

00:12:02.897 --> 00:12:03.730
- Yeah.

00:12:03.730 --> 00:12:08.147
There's a certain type of
dress that looks like this.

00:12:09.757 --> 00:12:10.776
Ridiculous tasting.

00:12:10.776 --> 00:12:13.952
- It kinda does me a favor in
picking up eating more curry.

00:12:13.952 --> 00:12:14.896
It goes to the cracks,

00:12:14.896 --> 00:12:15.992
so as I'm chewing it,

00:12:15.992 --> 00:12:17.368
it's oozing through my teeth.

00:12:17.368 --> 00:12:18.619
- [Andrew] Right off the bone.

00:12:18.619 --> 00:12:19.452
- [Steven] Wow!

00:12:19.452 --> 00:12:20.285
I'm getting chills right now.

00:12:20.285 --> 00:12:22.162
- [Andrew] And it's crazy
that we get so much pleasure

00:12:22.162 --> 00:12:24.495
from watching meat fall off a bone.

00:12:24.495 --> 00:12:25.328
- [Steven] Yeah.

00:12:25.328 --> 00:12:27.187
- Which in any other context would be

00:12:27.187 --> 00:12:29.104
a truly horrific image.

00:12:30.096 --> 00:12:33.846
- [Steven] This is crazy,
it looks so tender.

00:12:36.733 --> 00:12:38.715
I can't really pinpoint what the flavor is

00:12:38.715 --> 00:12:40.978
that it's like hitting me so hard.

00:12:40.978 --> 00:12:41.846
- That's what's good about it,

00:12:41.846 --> 00:12:43.832
you can't isolate any one flavor,

00:12:43.832 --> 00:12:46.249
it's just a bunch of flavors.

00:12:49.274 --> 00:12:50.607
It's really good

00:12:51.736 --> 00:12:52.569
- Oh!

00:12:52.776 --> 00:12:54.168
- So that's how you know it's good.

00:12:54.168 --> 00:12:56.258
Adam tasted it, thought it was good.

00:12:56.258 --> 00:12:58.265
Then took a few steps,
and then said "Oh!",

00:12:58.265 --> 00:13:01.653
because the flavor changed in
his mouth as he was eating it.

00:13:01.653 --> 00:13:04.254
- So for desserts we have the Shahi Tukra.

00:13:04.254 --> 00:13:05.574
And I don't think there's anyone

00:13:05.574 --> 00:13:08.494
better suited to describe
it and explain it

00:13:08.494 --> 00:13:10.003
than our pastry chef Gustavo.

00:13:10.003 --> 00:13:12.007
- We made a brioche bread
which is very buttery.

00:13:12.007 --> 00:13:14.647
Then we pan-fry it, we
soak it in a simple syrup

00:13:14.647 --> 00:13:15.931
that was made with saffron,

00:13:15.931 --> 00:13:18.666
which is one of the traditional
spice in Indian cuisine.

00:13:18.666 --> 00:13:19.960
We start putting in the rabri.

00:13:19.960 --> 00:13:22.039
So rabri is the reduction of milk.

00:13:22.039 --> 00:13:24.025
We put it on top of the Shahi Tukra.

00:13:24.025 --> 00:13:27.389
Cashews, pecans, pistachios,
edible rose petals,

00:13:27.389 --> 00:13:28.962
and then pop sorghum.

00:13:28.962 --> 00:13:29.955
And that's it.

00:13:29.955 --> 00:13:31.183
- [Steven] So we have a dessert wine here

00:13:31.183 --> 00:13:32.441
to pair with the dessert.

00:13:32.441 --> 00:13:33.274
- [Andrew] Ready?

00:13:33.274 --> 00:13:34.107
- Yeah.

00:13:35.857 --> 00:13:36.690
- Oh.

00:13:39.438 --> 00:13:40.271
Oh my God.

00:13:40.415 --> 00:13:42.998
A cool and floral french toast.

00:13:43.054 --> 00:13:44.368
- It's like got everything you want.

00:13:44.368 --> 00:13:45.801
Chocolate and butter.

00:13:45.801 --> 00:13:46.867
What the wine does,

00:13:46.867 --> 00:13:48.861
it kinda expands the floral taste

00:13:48.861 --> 00:13:50.770
of these dried rose petals.

00:13:50.770 --> 00:13:51.959
Adam, I would love for you

00:13:51.959 --> 00:13:53.916
to try this dessert with this wine.

00:13:53.916 --> 00:13:56.333
I think you're gonna love it.

00:13:57.241 --> 00:13:59.450
Okay Steven, New York was
a great place to try this,

00:13:59.450 --> 00:14:00.939
because we got to try curry

00:14:00.939 --> 00:14:02.780
from a lot of different
parts of the world.

00:14:02.780 --> 00:14:05.216
- So, Andrew, then which curry spot

00:14:05.216 --> 00:14:07.060
was the most worth it to you at its price?

00:14:07.060 --> 00:14:07.893
- Laut.

00:14:07.893 --> 00:14:09.663
I really appreciated his methodology.

00:14:09.663 --> 00:14:12.072
I love his attention to detail

00:14:12.072 --> 00:14:14.088
and his respect to history.

00:14:14.088 --> 00:14:17.180
- I think the thing that I
was most surprised by today

00:14:17.180 --> 00:14:20.853
was the shrimp, actually,
that was at Junoon.

00:14:20.853 --> 00:14:22.559
Ah I don't know my winner.

00:14:22.559 --> 00:14:26.015
I think my worth it winner, Fay Da Bakery.

00:14:26.015 --> 00:14:26.848
- Oh okay.

00:14:27.195 --> 00:14:28.814
- At least the value for this one.

00:14:28.814 --> 00:14:30.458
I love curry so much,

00:14:30.458 --> 00:14:32.438
and the ability to get it for two dollars.

00:14:32.438 --> 00:14:33.434
It's so accessible.

00:14:33.434 --> 00:14:34.829
- Annie, who's your worth it winner?

00:14:34.829 --> 00:14:35.662
- Junoon.

00:14:35.752 --> 00:14:36.585
- Laut.

00:14:39.388 --> 00:14:41.561
- Okay so that was a day in New York City.

00:14:41.561 --> 00:14:43.411
But it doesn't end there, because next,

00:14:43.411 --> 00:14:46.355
we'll be doing something else in New York.

00:14:46.355 --> 00:14:47.646
- Alright, so we're
going out tonight, right?

00:14:47.646 --> 00:14:48.479
- No.

00:14:49.046 --> 00:14:51.879
(energetic music)

00:14:54.081 --> 00:14:55.748
- [Narrator] Oh yes.

